BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Accelerating Research and Engineering with AI
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261119T163000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261119T173000
DTSTAMP:20261111T063824Z
UID:funcheap-1646734@sf-cal
SEQUENCE:0
CATEGORIES:Lectures Workshops
DESCRIPTION:Submitted by the Event OrganizerSimons InstituteLarge language
  models are now increasingly used to accelerate many research and engineer
 ing activities\, thanks to their broad world knowledge and ability to work
  with arbitrary data. They have had great success in programming and math\
 , and are being applied to biology\, chemistry\, and many other areas. But
  what are the strengths and limitations of AI for these tasks\, and how ca
 n we make it even better? Matei Zaharia will describe research from UC Ber
 keley and other groups on using AI for engineering and research\, includin
 g algorithms that use AI to search for better solutions to a problem (e.g.
 \, GEPA\, AlphaEvolve\, and AdaEvolve)\, and results researchers have been
  getting with them in computer science\, biology\, and other areas. In gen
 eral\, AI can be trained and applied effectively when solutions are easy t
 o verify\, but it has a much harder time when they aren’t (e.g.\, in che
 mistry and biology\, where experiments take days). We are also getting bet
 ter\, however\, at translating more problems into the easy-to-verify setti
 ng. In this Theoretically Speaking public lecture\, Zaharia will discuss w
 here this might lead\, based on the trends we are seeing so far\, and how 
 science and engineering might look in the future when we have highly effec
 tive\, tireless AI coworkers to explore solutions for us.\nMatei Zaharia i
 s an Associate Professor of EECS at UC Berkeley and Co-Founder and Chief T
 echnology Officer of Databricks. He is best known as the...\n\nFree\n\nTic
 kets: https://simons.berkeley.edu/events/accelerating-research-engineering
 -ai-theoretically-speaking\nDetails: https://sf.funcheap.com/accelerating-
 research-engineering-ai/\nSource: Funcheap SF
IMAGE;VALUE=URI:https://cdn.funcheap.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/11/Matei-
 Zaharia-web-graphic.png.jpg
LAST-MODIFIED:20261111T063824Z
LOCATION:Simons Institute for the Theory of Computing\, UC Berkeley\, Melv
 in Calvin Laboratory\, Berkeley 94720
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
URL:https://sf.funcheap.com/accelerating-research-engineering-ai/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Clift Royal Sonesta Hotel Continues Its Speaker Series: Making
  History Fun Again
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261119T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261119T190000
DTSTAMP:20261104T195100Z
UID:dothebay-17973354@sf-cal
SEQUENCE:2
CATEGORIES:Lecture/Education
DESCRIPTION:The Clift Royal Sonesta Hotel\, a San Francisco landmark since
  1915\, continues its monthly speaker series\, Making History Fun Again\, 
 with “San Francisco’s Media Evolution\,” a conversation with journal
 ists and media leaders who have helped tell the city’s stories across ge
 nerations and platforms.\nOn Thursday\, November 19\, Heather Knight\, San
  Francisco bureau chief for The New York Times\, will moderate a panel fea
 turing Will Hearst\, editor and publisher of Alta Journal\; award-winning 
 television journalist Wendy Tokuda\; political reporter Joe Fitzgerald Rod
 riguez of The San Francisco Standard\; and radio host and political commen
 tator John F. Rothmann.\nFrom newspaper dispatches to television broadcast
 s\, radio conversations and news delivered through smartphones\, San Franc
 isco’s media have chronicled a city defined by reinvention. Earthquakes\
 , local politics\, cultural movements\, housing challenges and unforgettab
 le personalities have provided generations of journalists with stories tha
 t resonate far beyond the Bay Area.\nBringing together perspectives from p
 rint\, television\, digital news and radio\, the evening will explore how 
 the city’s stories reach audiences\, how journalism has changed and what
  endures as new technologies reshape the media landscape. Guests will hear
  from people who have brought Bay Area news into homes\, onto doorsteps\, 
 over the airwaves and into the palm of a hand.\nThe complimentary event be
 gins with non-hosted cocktails at 5:00 p.m.\, followed by the program fro.
 ..\n\nFree\n\nDetails: https://dothebay.com/events/2026/11/19/the-clift-ro
 yal-sonesta-hotel-continues-its-speaker-series-making-history-fun-again-ti
 ckets\nSource: DoTheBay\n\nAlso listed on:\n- https://sf.funcheap.com/clif
 t-royal-sonesta-hotel-speaker-series-making-history-fun-5/
GEO:37.786797;-122.41115
